<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.3.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02376</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.3.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>de7aef3b-a222-487f-8d26-70e67bb3733f</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="ce344af3-e2fc-47e5-89a0-723db7a50b6f" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>For the expressions in questions 1 – 4, choose which number would make the expression a perfect square. Then, choose an equivalent expression in factored form.</para><para>\(x^2+3x\)</para>
      <para>What number can be added to make \(x^2+3x\) a perfect square?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(\frac23\)</para><para>\(\frac49\)</para><para>\(\frac94\)</para><para>\(-\frac23\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(\frac94\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.3.2 and 9.3.3. </para><para>The correct answer is: \(\frac94\).
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="7824a5c3-ccf6-4a27-a7c5-40d1d790cff9" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Which expression is equivalent to \(x^2+3x\)?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(x(x + 3)\)</para><para>\(x(3x)\)</para><para>\((x + 3)(x + 3)\)</para><para>\(3(x + 1)\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(x(x + 3)\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.3.2 and 9.3.3. </para><para>The correct answer is: \(x(x + 3)\).
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="997a5371-4e56-4e04-ba7f-782c4c875a94" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(x^2+0.6x\)</para>
      <para> What number can be added to make \(x^2+0.6x\) a perfect square?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>0.09</para><para>0.81</para><para>0.36</para><para>0.03</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! 0.09</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.3.2 and 9.3.3. </para><para>The correct answer is: 0.09. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="c6f1b4c5-0f89-4180-af96-32c5f02c5af2" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Which expression is equivalent to \(x^2+0.6x\)?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(x(x+0.6x)\)</para><para>\(x(x+0.6)\)</para><para>\(x(0.6x)\)</para><para>\(0.6(x+1)\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(x(x+0.6)\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.3.2 and 9.3.3. </para><para>The correct answer is: \(x(x+0.6)\).
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="844310e2-1516-4923-98b9-0f1c94bbd63a" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(x^2-11x\)</para>
      <para>What number can be added to make \(x^2-11x\) a perfect square?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(\frac{11}{2}\)</para><para>\(\frac{25}{2}\)</para><para>\(\frac52\)</para><para>\(\frac{121}{4}\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(\frac{121}{4}\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.3.2 and 9.3.3. </para><para>The correct answer is: \(\frac{121}{4}\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="25442b64-c58b-4126-9f4b-9d092761dc32" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Which expression is equivalent to \(x^2-11x\)?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(x(x-11x)\)</para><para>\(x(x-11)\)</para><para>\(11(x-1)\)</para><para>\(11x(x-1)\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(x(x-11)\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.3.2 and 9.3.3. </para><para>The correct answer is: \(x(x-11)\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="8cca33a0-b21b-4a19-8aa6-b42f50a7644c" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(x^2-\frac{5}{2}x\)</para>
      <para> What number can be added to make \(x^2-\frac{5}{2}x\) a perfect square?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(\frac{25}{4}\)</para><para>\(\frac{25}{16}\)</para><para>\(\frac58\)</para><para>\(\frac68\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(\frac{25}{16}\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.3.2 and 9.3.3. </para><para>The correct answer is: \(\frac{25}{16}\).
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="d10954da-8650-4ab4-9d0c-a6a6f2383aa1" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Which expression is equivalent to \(x^2-\frac{5}{2}x\)?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(x(x-\frac52)\)</para><para>\(x(x-\frac{5}{2}x)\)</para><para>\(\frac{5}{2}x(x-1)\)</para><para>\(\frac{5}{2}(x-1)\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(x(x-\frac52)\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.2.2 and 9.2.3. </para><para>The correct answer is: \(x(x-\frac52)\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="feefda53-6a1a-4443-95d3-5229cbb484a1" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What number can be added to make \(x^2+x\) a perfect square?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(\frac14\)</para><para>\(\frac12\)</para><para>1</para><para>0</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(\frac14\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.2.2 and 9.2.3. </para><para>The correct answer is: \(\frac14\).
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="c4829eed-28cd-415d-b5d2-48483676a0b1" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What number can be added to make \(x^2-\frac{3}{2}x\) a perfect square?  </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(\frac23\)</para><para>\(\frac49\)</para><para>\(\frac{9}{16}\)</para><para>\(-\frac23\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(\frac{9}{16}\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.2.2 and 9.2.3. </para><para>The correct answer is: \(\frac{9}{16}\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="e3c26867-40b6-4860-8b80-1204ffe62885" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Noah is solving the equation \(x^2+8x+15=3\). He begins by rewriting the expression on the left in factored form and writes \((x+3)(x+5)=3\). He does not know what to do next. </para>
      <para>Do you think using the factored form is the best way for Noah to solve this equation?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! No. Noah should use completing the square.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 9.3.2. </para><para>The correct answer is No. Noah should use completing the square. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="b310b720-bf24-4fbd-9217-83fe38560355" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Solve the original equation, then choose the<emphasis> two</emphasis> roots.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(x=-2\) and \(x=-6\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.2.2 and 9.2.3. </para><para>The correct answers are: \(x=-2\) and \(x=-6\).
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="e1a27da5-2ce6-41c4-a658-1abd528f4ea3" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>An equation and its solutions are given. Solve the equation by completing the square, then choose whether you agree or disagree with the given solutions.</para>
      <para>\(x^2+20x+50=14\). The solutions are \(x=-18\) and \(x=-2\).</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! Agree. The correct solutions are \(x=-18\) and \(x=-2\).</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.3.3 and 9.3.4. </para><para>The correct answer is Disagree. The correct solutions are \(x=-18\) and \(x=-2\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="8d585fd8-2607-4e8f-af9d-0fb10aeea7a4" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(x^2+1.6x=0.36\). The solutions are \(x=-1.8\) and \(x=0.2\).</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! Agree. \(x=-1.8\) and \(x=0.2\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.3.3 and 9.3.4. </para><para>The correct answer is Agree. \(x=-1.8\) and \(x=0.2\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="3993561c-b0a9-4e42-af8b-5827ff6f7933" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(x^2-5x=\frac{11}{4}\). The solutions are \(x=\frac{11}{4}\) and \(x=-12\).</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! Disagree. The correct solutions are \(x=\frac{11}{2}\) and \(x=-\frac12\).</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.3.3 and 9.3.4. </para><para>The correct answer is Disagree. The correct solutions are \(x=\frac{11}{2}\) and \(x=-\frac12\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="e510a689-c1be-4c61-b360-d98732756763" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> Solve each equation, then choose the <emphasis>two</emphasis> roots.</para>
      <para>\(x^2-0.5x=0.5\)</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(x=-0.5\) and \(x=1\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.3.2 – 9.3.4. </para><para>The correct answers are: \(x=-0.5\) and \(x=1\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="27b99384-bd9f-47a5-aed0-2d1cbeafe84d" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(x^2+0.8x=0.09\)</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(x=-0.9\) and \(x=0.1\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.3.2 – 9.3.4. </para><para>The correct answers are: \(x=-0.9\) and \(x=0.1\).
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="6c1881a7-7b81-4541-ba80-46f685e9b9ba" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(x^2+\frac{13}{3}x=\frac{56}{36}\)</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(x=-\frac{14}{3}\) and \(x = \frac{1}{3}\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.3.2 – 9.3.4. </para><para>The correct answers are: \(x=-\frac{14}{3}\) and \(x = \frac{1}{3}\).
      </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
</content>
